        "id": 1000000
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-09-11T06:46:40Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e \u003e I don\u0027t see how this could be called twice for the exact same instance of the same plugin?\n\u003e \n\u003e I agree that, in the normal startup flow, these two paths should be mutually exclusive. If the broker is already bound, `start()` calls `onBrokerApiStarted()`, otherwise, the `BrokerApiPluginListener` calls it once the broker is bound.\n\u003e \n\u003e My concern is that all of this is relying exclusively on this lifecycle ordering, and it is not enforced in the class.\n\nThe lifecycle ordering is enforced in Gerrit core, though.\nThis plugin doesn\u0027t have any lifecycle outside of Gerrit.\nWe can put an assertion about the impossible situation instead of making it idempotent.\n\n\u003e If it was ever called twice, we would register the same consumer twice with the same group ID. What happens after that would then depend on the concrete broker implementation, I believe.\n\nAn assertion would cover that impossible case anyway.\n\n\u003e I still think it would be safer to make that operation idempotent (an `AtomicBoolean` should be enough?). That would also make the lifecycle easier to reason about if the broker is stopped, restarted, or reloaded, in my opinion.\n\nI disagree: we are writing code for a situation that will never happen. If that impossible situation happens, we should throw an exception loud and clear.",
      "parentUuid": "f50a0698_8d525362",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 82,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 82,
        "endChar": 27
      },
